WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.291 --> 00:00:06.080
F3: There's a new report that the Biden&nbsp;
administration is planning what is being called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.120
the biggest tax increase in thirty years. Now, I&nbsp;
want to be really careful in how I report this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:17.280
because, first of all, tax increase is a relative&nbsp;
term in that much of what Joe Biden wants to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:26.400
would be to undo, in some cases only some&nbsp;
of the Republican tax decreases of the past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.960
decades. Also, none of what is being reported&nbsp;
is outside of what Joe Biden promised to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:40.640
So we're seeing all of these hot takes about&nbsp;
shocking betrayal by Biden of I don't know what,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:48.080
because he told us when he ran what his tax plan&nbsp;
was going to be. And the reports from Bloomberg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:55.520
yesterday, which broke this story, that Joe&nbsp;
Biden's next big project is tax reform. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:02.240
the stuff he told us he was going to do. So let's&nbsp;
look at it. The reported Biden tax plan priorities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:08.960
are to increase the income tax rate on people&nbsp;
making more than 400000 dollars a year. Remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:15.920
that's earned income separate from capital gains,&nbsp;
which we will get to. Joe Biden would expand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:21.600
estate tax, which we don't have the details yet.&nbsp;
You could expand the estate tax in two ways. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:29.200
could make it kick in at a lower level of wealth,&nbsp;
or you could increase the rate at which estates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:36.080
are taxed. Joe Biden will. This is the report from&nbsp;
Bloomberg says Joe Biden will look to reduce tax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:42.000
preferences on pass through businesses,&nbsp;
which would affect The David Pakman Show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:49.280
would increase to a higher capital gains tax rate&nbsp;
for people making over one million dollars per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.840
year would increase the corporate tax rate&nbsp;
from twenty one to twenty eight percent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.520
And in all likelihood, even if he were&nbsp;
to move on this very soon, nothing would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:07.840
take effect until at least 2022 as a tax year,&nbsp;
meaning that you would deal with these changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:14.640
sometime between January and April 15th of&nbsp;
twenty twenty three. That that's right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.680
And of course, that may change. Now, let's&nbsp;
just pick one of these, because so much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.800
this is being is being sort of misunderstood. The&nbsp;
corporate tax rate. What Joe Biden wants to do is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.160
increase it from twenty one to twenty&nbsp;
eight percent, according to this report.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:34.640
It's important to remember that the corporate&nbsp;
tax rate, number one, used to be in the mid 40s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:40.880
and then it dropped to the low 40s than 35 percent&nbsp;
and it stood at 35 for a very long time. Then it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:48.000
went to twenty one under Trump. So what Joe Biden&nbsp;
would do would be to only go partially back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.760
longstanding corporate tax rate of 35 percent by&nbsp;
going to twenty eight. It's not exactly radical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:59.120
And then just as importantly, there's so many&nbsp;
people saying going to twenty eight percent will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.320
hurt small businesses. This is a fundamental&nbsp;
misunderstanding. Of most small businesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:11.040
most small businesses, if you're a sole&nbsp;
proprietor, if you are an LLC or an S corporation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.960
this doesn't affect you because all of those&nbsp;
business entities are taxed at the personal rate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:23.120
What I mean by that is if you have a personal&nbsp;
housecleaning business and you make sixty thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:29.360
dollars, you don't pay a twenty one or twenty&nbsp;
eight or 35 percent corporate tax rate. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:36.160
are taxed on that sixty thousand dollars on the&nbsp;
personal income tax rates. Now my prediction is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.000
that politically a lot of this stuff is going&nbsp;
to be really tough right now because of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.840
pandemic. And we'll talk to Bill Share about&nbsp;
this a little bit later on on the program.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:52.080
Of course, Republicans are all going to say we&nbsp;
should never do tax increases, but in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:57.200
we shouldn't do them right now because of&nbsp;
the pandemic. There are also going to be some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.840
Democrats who will make that case, particularly&nbsp;
in the Senate. I do think that increasing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:08.080
corporate tax rate is the easiest thing to do&nbsp;
that Joe Biden is proposing. I believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:14.400
the other aspects of this are going to see&nbsp;
significant political difficulty. I'm not saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.520
it shouldn't happen. I am merely giving you my&nbsp;
opinion of what I predict is going to happen now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:26.400
We talked about some of the changes that that are&nbsp;
going to be made there in terms of pass-through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:32.400
entities and different things, the tax plan.&nbsp;
Joe Biden is proposing, if it happens, is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:38.560
to lead to me as the owner of The David Pakman&nbsp;
Show, paying more in taxes. And I'm OK with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:45.200
as long as the money is being put to good use,&nbsp;
which it already is not. And as I've said before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:51.680
many times we could and should be rethinking our&nbsp;
spending priorities now. And we may not even have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.240
to increase taxes to fund the programs that are&nbsp;
important to us. That's separate from whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:02.240
billionaires and millionaires should be paying&nbsp;
more and and they should. But of the roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:07.120
one point five trillion dollars in discretionary&nbsp;
spending that the federal government does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:14.160
almost half of it is for the military. What?&nbsp;
That's insane. So I'm not saying have no military.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:20.240
I'm not saying be weak. I'm not saying anything&nbsp;
other than half of our discretionary spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:25.200
shouldn't be going to the military. And that's a&nbsp;
conversation. That is a conversation that must be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:31.760
had regardless of what the tax rate is on whoever.&nbsp;
Now, the question here is less an economic one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:37.040
than a political one. We know that Joe Biden's&nbsp;
plan would be deficit negative. It would raise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.840
more money than it would cost that that's been&nbsp;
analyzed by the Tax Policy Center. We know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:49.040
the question is going to be my instinct is&nbsp;
that you are going to see Republicans and some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:54.560
Democrats come together and say it's not the right&nbsp;
time to do this. Let's see how it goes. For now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.560 --> 00:06:01.120
the White House is simply saying, without&nbsp;
commenting on the Bloomberg report, anything we do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.640
would not increase the taxes of people making&nbsp;
under four hundred thousand dollars per year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:13.760
That's the statement from the White House so far,&nbsp;
